course: dictionary
lesson: 5308
title: Kayak: a boat you paddle
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A kayak is a light, narrow boat for one or two people. You sit inside it and move it along with a paddle that has a blade at each end.
explain: Context helps you here. «A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards: level, radar, kayak, racecar.» Kayak is in a list of words that read the same both ways, so you can check it: k-a-y-a-k backwards is k-a-y-a-k.
explain: Kayak can also be a verb, meaning to travel in a kayak. «We kayak on the lake every summer» means we go out in a kayak every summer. You may also see phrases like «go kayaking» and «sea kayak».
fact: A kayak is a light, narrow boat for one or two people, with a covered top.
fact: A kayak is moved with a paddle that has a blade at each end.
fact: Kayaks were first made by Inuit and other Arctic peoples, from wood and seal skin, for hunting.
fact: Always wear a life jacket (buoyancy aid), go with an adult, and check the weather before you go kayaking.
fact: «Kayak» is a palindrome: it reads the same forwards and backwards.
exercise: What is a kayak? | A big ship with sails and a heavy engine | *A light, narrow boat for one or two people, moved with a paddle | A kind of bicycle for use on water
exercise: Which of these words reads the same forwards and backwards? | paddle | *level | happy | snow
exercise: The Arctic peoples first made kayaks for what purpose? | Racing in the Olympics | *Hunting | Carrying cargo across deserts | Growing crops
test: Before you go kayaking, what should you always do? | Skip the safety rules to go faster | Swim out as far as you can | *Wear a life jacket and check the weather | Go alone at night, because it is quieter
test: In «We kayak on the lake every summer», what word class is kayak? | A verb: to swim under water | A noun: a large lake in Canada | *A verb: to travel in a kayak | A noun: a paddle with a blade at each end
exam: How is a kayak moved along? | With a sail on a mast | *With a paddle that has a blade at each end | With a motor at the back | By pushing a long pole against the riverbed
